C++ `for` loops follow the classic C format — initialization, condition, update:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    std::cout << i << std::endl;   // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

The `while` loop runs while a condition holds:

```cpp
int count = 0;
while (count < 3) {
    std::cout << "Tick " << count << std::endl;
    count++;
}
```

The **range-based for** (C++11+) loops directly over collections:

```cpp
int nums[] = {10, 20, 30};
for (int n : nums) {
    std::cout << n << std::endl;
}
```

Loop controls:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 2) continue;   // skip this iteration
    if (i == 5) break;      // exit the loop
    std::cout << i << std::endl;
}
```

Count down with `i--`:

```cpp
for (int i = 3; i > 0; i--) {
    std::cout << i << std::endl;   // 3 2 1
}
```

